  <h1>Contributing Skills Content</h1>
  <p>
    Skills are the configuration-only way to teach a role something new — a workflow, a project convention, a
    linting process. Because a skill is packaged instructions rather than system code, contributing good skill content
    is one of the lightest and most accessible ways to help the project.
  </p>

  <h2>How skills work</h2>
  <p>
    A skill is a set of instructions a role can load on demand. It typically captures a repeatable capability — how
    this project runs its checks, the conventions we follow for this kind of work, the steps for that process — and it
    is scoped per role, so the builder and the reviewer can carry different skills. Each skill is configured into a
    role through the team configuration, without any system code change.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the full walkthrough of what a skill is and how a role picks one up, see the
    <a href="/docs/guides/extending-skills">Adding a Skill</a> guide.
  </p>

  <h2>Contributing skills content</h2>
  <p>
    From a contributor’s perspective, a skill contribution is a documentation-and-content change:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Describe the capability clearly.</strong> State what outcome the skill produces, when a role should use it, and what inputs and outputs it needs.</li>
    <li><strong>Write precise instructions.</strong> A good skill is specific enough to be followed deterministically and includes a worked example.</li>
    <li><strong>Document failure modes.</strong> What can go wrong, how to detect it, and what the role should do next.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep it scoped.</strong> One skill does one thing. If a contribution needs to change how the system itself behaves, it’s a core feature, not a skill.</li>
  </ul>

  <Callout type="info" title="No code change required">
    Adding skill content should never require touching system code. If your proposal needs that, it isn’t a skill — it
    belongs in a core-feature discussion instead. Align on the approach before investing in the content.
  </Callout>

  <h2>How a skill contribution enters the project</h2>
  <p>
    A skills-content contribution follows the same review-before-merge path as any other change: check existing work to
    avoid duplication, make a focused change, keep quality high, and open a pull request that explains what you added
    and why. Maintainers review it with the project’s standards in mind before merging.
  </p>

